---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { MEASURED_ON, axes, libsignalReadme, notes, projects } from '../lib/comparison.mjs';

/*
 * The page that answers "why does this exist when libsignal exists".
 *
 * That question gets asked in every thread, and the answer is not ours to make
 * — it is in libsignal's own README, which is why the quote is the first thing
 * on the page and not a pull-quote two thirds of the way down a homepage. A
 * sentence the maintainers wrote about their own project carries weight nothing
 * we could write would.
 *
 * The page is built to be checked rather than believed. Every figure carries
 * the date it was measured, every project links to its repository, and the two
 * best-maintained alternatives are described as well-maintained because they
 * are. A comparison page that concludes "use ours" on every axis is read as
 * marketing; one that says "if MLS suits your product, use ts-mls" is read as
 * information, and it is also true.
 *
 * The data is in src/lib/comparison.mjs so /product and the homepage summarise
 * the same claims rather than each keeping a copy that drifts.
 */
const byKey = Object.fromEntries(notes.map((note) => [note.key, note]));
const ordered = projects.map((project) => ({ project, note: byKey[project.key] }));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it compares"
  description="The Signal Protocol libraries available in JavaScript, measured rather than asserted: libsignal, libsignal-protocol-javascript, @privacyresearch/libsignal-protocol-typescript, ts-mls, and this SDK, with every figure dated and every project linked."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">How it compares</p>
      <h1>The case for this SDK is a sentence someone else wrote.</h1>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>{libsignalReadme.quote}</blockquote>
        <figcaption>
          <a href={libsignalReadme.href}>{libsignalReadme.attribution}</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="lead">
        libsignal is excellent, actively maintained, and the most scrutinised Signal Protocol code
        there is. It is also a native Node addon that does not run in a browser, in Expo, or in
        React Native, and its maintainers say plainly that use outside Signal is not something they
        support. That is the gap. Below is every alternative we know of, measured on {MEASURED_ON}.
      </p>
      <p class="maturity">0.1.x alpha — public APIs and persisted formats may change before 1.0.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Every option, on the axes that decide it</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          Read from the GitHub API, the npm registry API, and the published package tarballs on
          {' '}{MEASURED_ON}. Dates are the ones those APIs returned. The axes are the ones this SDK
          was built to change, so the table is not a quality ranking — and the column for our own
          package is the one column nobody independent measured.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">&nbsp;</th>
              {projects.map((project) => (
                <th scope="col">
                  {project.ours ? (
                    <code>{project.name}</code>
                  ) : (
                    <a href={project.href}><code>{project.name}</code></a>
                  )}
                </th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {axes.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.axis}</th>
                {row.cells.map((cell) => <td>{cell}</td>)}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted measure">
        A licence row is not a scorecard either. <code>ts-mls</code> is MIT, which already permits
        proprietary use, so &ldquo;no commercial licence offered&rdquo; costs its users nothing.
        Ours is AGPL-3.0-or-later with a commercial licence available, which is a cost to some
        readers and the reason the row is there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>What each one is actually for</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          A table flattens the thing a table is worst at: what a project is good at. Two of the five
          below end with a recommendation that is not this SDK.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        {ordered.map(({ project, note }) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {note.heading} —{' '}
              {project.ours ? (
                <code>{project.name}</code>
              ) : (
                <a href={project.href}><code>{project.name}</code></a>
              )}
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <p>{note.body}</p>
              <p class="verdict">{note.verdict}</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container measure stack">
      <h2>The rest of that README paragraph</h2>
      <p>
        The sentence at the top of this page is usually quoted on its own. The one after it is the
        practical half, and it is the reason &ldquo;unsupported&rdquo; matters to anyone planning a
        roadmap rather than reading a licence.
      </p>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>{libsignalReadme.continuation}</blockquote>
        <figcaption>
          <a href={libsignalReadme.href}>{libsignalReadme.attribution}</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        That is a reasonable position for a project whose job is to serve one application very well.
        It is a difficult position to build a product on top of, and this SDK exists because we
        wanted the other thing: a Signal Protocol implementation whose supported case is your app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container measure stack">
      <h2>Check it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing above should be taken on our word. Every project links to its repository, every date
        came from a public API on {MEASURED_ON}, and the commands that read them are in the comment
        at the top of <code>src/lib/comparison.mjs</code>. The SDK README carries a shorter version
        of the same table.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
          <span class="oe-button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
          <span class="cta-sublabel">ten minutes · two clients · no account</span>
        </a>
        <a class="oe-button oe-button-secondary" href="https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js#how-it-compares">
          The shorter table in the SDK README
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/product">See the SDK surface</a>
        <a href="/security">The security review pack</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
